    "rationale": "Resubmission of the twice-accepted optimal stream (rounds 2 and 3, 46 commands, 4096 input bytes). M=16 is one row block; N=80 splits into 5 column blocks and K=32 into 2 K-steps of 16. Scratchpad rows 0-15 hold the current A K-chunk and rows 16-31 the current B tile, so only one A chunk is resident at a time; the 32-row accumulator holds at most two pending 16-row block results, so N is processed in groups {0,1},{2,3},{4}. Per group: with A K-chunk 0 resident, compute each block's K-chunk 0 in overwrite mode (bit30=0) into distinct accumulator halves; then load A K-chunk 1 and compute each block's K-chunk 1 in accumulate mode (bit30=1) onto the same rows, giving each output cell its two products in increasing K order; then mvout both blocks. Every B tile (2 K-steps x 5 N-blocks) is DMA'd exactly once (10 tiles) and A chunks are loaded once per group (3 x 2 = 6 tiles), the minimum since chunk0 and chunk1 computes cannot overlap in the 32-row scratchpad, the single latched-B slot cannot survive across an interleaved block's compute, and partials evicted from the accumulator cannot be read back. Total 16 mvin tiles = 4096 input bytes, the information-theoretic floor given these residency constraints. All computes use accumulated=false with preload-staged B tiles, bd_spad_addr is always GARBAGE_ADDR, every out_addr/acc_addr sets bit 31 (accumulator) and bit 29 (full C width), and bit 30 distinguishes accumulate (3758096384/3758096400) from overwrite (2684354560/2684354576). Five mvouts store the finished 16x16 blocks at C element offsets 16j with store stride 320 bytes, followed by a final fence; 46 commands is minimal since 10 computes (one per K-step/N-block tile product) each require a preload, 16 mvins and 5 mvouts are forced, plus 4 configs and a fence."
